v class=\"item-description\">\n   525→                        Security researchers discovered that Meta's AI support chatbot could be tricked into granting unauthorized access to Instagram accounts.\n   526→                    </div>\n   527→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   528→                        By <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/\">Lorenzo Franceschi-Bicchierai</a> | 2026-06-01\n   529→                    </div>\n   530→                    <div class=\"source\">TechCrunch</div>\n   531→                    <div class=\"topics\">\n   532→                        <span class=\"topic\">AI Security</span>\n   533→                        <span class=\"topic\">Meta</span>\n   534→                        <span class=\"topic\">Social Engineering</span>\n   535→                    </div>\n   536→                </div>\n   537→\n   538→                <div class=\"item\">\n   539→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   540→                        <span class=\"type-indicator type-opinion\"></span>\n   541→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Jun/5/andreas-kling/\">Andreas Kling on Ladybird Development</a>\n   542→                    </div>\n   543→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   544→                        Andreas Kling discusses development philosophy changes for the Ladybird browser project, with commentary on AI ethics and open-source development.\n   545→                    </div>\n   546→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   547→                        By <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/\">Simon Willison</a> | 2026-06-05\n   548→                    </div>\n   549→                    <div class=\"source\">Blog</div>\n   550→                    <div class=\"topics\">\n   551→                        <span class=\"topic\">Open Source</span>\n   552→                        <span class=\"topic\">AI Ethics</span>\n   553→                        <span class=\"topic\">Browser Development</span>\n   554→                    </div>\n   555→                </div>\n   556→\n   557→                <div class=\"item\">\n   558→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   559→                        <span class=\"type-indicator type-opinion\"></span>\n   560→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Jun/4/a-slightly-different-version/\">Google's AI Statement Changes</a>\n   561→                    </div>\n   562→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   563→                        404 Media reports that Google asked to change their statement, removing the phrase about keeping humans in the loop, raising oversight concerns.\n   564→                    </div>\n   565→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   566→                        By <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/\">Simon Willison</a> | 2026-06-04\n   567→                    </div>\n   568→                    <div class=\"source\">Blog</div>\n   569→                    <div class=\"topics\">\n   570→                        <span class=\"topic\">AI Ethics</span>\n   571→                        <span class=\"topic\">Google</span>\n   572→                        <span class=\"topic\">AI Oversight</span>\n   573→                    </div>\n   574→                </div>\n   575→            </section>\n   576→        </main>\n   577→\n   578→        <footer>\n   579→            <a href=\"#\">Previous Reports</a>\n   580→            <a href=\"#\">Methodology</a>\n   581→            <a href=\"#\">About</a>\n   582→            <a href=\"#\">Subscribe</a>\n   583→            <span>|</span>\n   584→            <span>Generated with Claude Code</span>\n   585→        </footer>\n   586→    </div>\n   587→</body>\n   588→</html>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
